# Findings already raised with whoever can fix them. They stay in every report;
# they simply stop setting the "needs attention" exit code, so a scheduled run
# is quiet until something NEW goes wrong.
#
# An alert that fires every night gets muted, and then it costs you the one that
# matters. This file is what keeps that from happening — and it is a record of
# "we know, it is being handled", not a place to hide findings.
#
# Copy to acknowledged.txt and edit. Two kinds of entry, and they do not
# collide:
#
#   1. A hostname          — that device's collection failure is known
#   2. A finding key       — that specific policy finding is known
#
# Finding keys are printed by `netconfig-mirror audit` when it exits non-zero;
# copy them from there rather than typing them.

# Say where each group was raised and when. A suppression file with no dates
# and no reference is indistinguishable from one where things were swept under
# the carpet — and whoever opens it in six months needs somewhere to go to find
# out what happened.

# A device is acknowledged by the name the run reports for it: its hostname,
# or its address when the collector never identified it.

# --- Devices storing a 65-byte error string instead of a configuration.
# The collector reports these as successfully collected.
# Raised with the collector team 2026-09-12.
UK-LD5-R316-RTR1
UK-LD5-R316-RTR2

# --- Routing policy findings.
#
# Generate these rather than typing them — one wrong character and the
# acknowledgement silently stops matching, and the finding alerts again with
# nothing to explain why:
#
#     docker/run.sh audit --keys >> docker/acknowledged.txt
#
# That prints only findings not yet acknowledged, so it is safe to re-run: it
# appends what is new and nothing else.
#
# Read what it appended before committing it. Every line means "this is known
# and somebody is dealing with it" — a finding parked here that nobody is
# actually working on is worse than not having the file at all, because it is
# now invisible and looks handled.
#
# Format, for reference:
#   dangling:<hostname>:<what refers>:<name it refers to>
#
# Raised in <report>, <date>. Update this line when the status changes.
